<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Lagos</h1><p class="st-meta">1600 CE – 1861 CE (approx.) · capital Lagos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Yoruba island kingdom under the authority of Benin that became a major 19th century slave port before Britain annexed it in 1861.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a>, known locally as Eko, began as a settlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awori">Awori</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> on the lagoon islands. In the 16th or early 17th century the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/benin-empire">Benin Empire</a> established a war camp there, and Ashipa, recognised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba of Benin</a>, founded the line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-of-lagos">Obas of Lagos</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> ruled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> white-cap chiefs and paid tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a> grew into a leading slave port in the late 18th and early 19th centuries. A succession struggle between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosoko">Kosoko</a> and his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akitoye">Akitoye</a> drew in the British, who bombarded Lagos in 1851, deposed Kosoko and installed Akitoye.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under pressure from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dosunmu">Dosunmu</a> signed a treaty ceding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a> in 1861, and it became <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lagos-colony">Lagos Colony</a>. Lagos grew into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria's</a> largest city, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-of-lagos">Oba of Lagos</a> remains its traditional ruler.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1600 CE – 1861 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:91.54%;width:5.19%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1600 CE – 1861 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lagos</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ashipa, Kosoko, Dosunmu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Yoruba religion and Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Yoruba</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Eko, Lagos</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1730&amp;focus=kingdom-of-lagos" aria-label="Kingdom of Lagos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4774 1936 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Lagos lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5094 2192L5155 2197M5094 2192L5094 2192"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5155 2197h0M5094 2192h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5094 2192h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5094 2192h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lagos. <a href="/atlas?year=1730&amp;focus=kingdom-of-lagos">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
